  <data key="d2">chunk-8a86011fbed05d0890a350d2b791ea51</data>
</node>
<node id="Derivative Matrix">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">The derivative matrix, also known as the Jacobian matrix, is a matrix of all first-order partial derivatives of a vector-valued function, which is relevant in the study of multi-variable calculus.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8a86011fbed05d0890a350d2b791ea51</data>
</node>
<node id="Single Variable Function">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A single variable function is a function that depends on one variable, and it is used to illustrate the concept of differentiability and linear approximation in calculus.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8a86011fbed05d0890a350d2b791ea51</data>
</node>
<node id="Multi Variable Function">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A multi variable function is a function that depends on more than one variable, and it is used to illustrate the concept of partial derivatives and linear approximation in multi-variable calculus.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-8a86011fbed05d0890a350d2b791ea51</data>
</node>
<node id="Jacobian Matrix">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">The Jacobian matrix is a mathematical concept that represents the gradient of a vector-valued function with respect to its input variables, providing a linear approximation of the function's behavior in the neighborhood of a given point.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="Jacobian Determinant">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">The Jacobian determinant is the determinant of a Jacobian matrix, which is used to determine if the mapping between input and output spaces is invertible.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="linear approximation">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">Linear approximation is a method used to approximate the value of a function near a given point using the linear mapping provided by the Jacobian matrix.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="vector-valued function">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">A vector-valued function is a function that maps an input to a vector in a vector space, and its Jacobian matrix captures the linear transformation it induces.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="input space">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">Input space refers to the set of all possible input values for a function, and the Jacobian matrix describes how the function transforms these inputs into outputs.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="output space">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">Output space refers to the set of all possible output values for a function, and the Jacobian matrix describes the linear mapping from input space to output space.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="derivative">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">Derivative is a fundamental concept in calculus that measures the rate at which a quantity is changing with respect to another quantity.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="gradient">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">Gradient is a mathematical concept that describes the rate of change of a function with respect to a variable.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient is a vector that points in the direction of the steepest increase of a scalar function of multiple variables.&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient is the direction of fastest increase of the objective function, denoted as ∇f(x).&lt;SEP&gt;Gradient is the vector of partial derivatives of a multivariable function, which is represented by the Jacobian matrix, indicating the direction of the steepest ascent of the function.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2204ffef1e97e333dfc5498edbd5ae7b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5b1d8c9e7343cf20cf0c57d99836ff7c</data>
</node>
<node id="marginal change">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">Marginal change refers to the change in the output of a function due to a small change in its input variables, which can be calculated using the Jacobian matrix.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="m × n matrix">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">A m × n matrix is a rectangular array of numbers or symbols arranged in rows and columns, used to represent the Jacobian matrix in the context of linear transformations.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="output variable">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">An output variable is a quantity that depends on one or more input variables and is represented by each row of the Jacobian matrix, capturing the rate of change of the function with respect to the input variables.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="partial derivatives">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">Partial derivatives are the derivatives of a function with respect to one of its variables, holding the other variables constant, and they are used to construct the Jacobian matrix.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="function">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">A function is a relation between a set of inputs and a set of permissible outputs, where each input is related to exactly one output, and the Jacobian matrix represents the linear approximation of this function.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="input variable">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">An input variable is a variable that the function depends on, and its partial derivatives are used to form the Jacobian matrix, which describes the function's behavior with respect to the input variables.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="linear transformation">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">A linear transformation is a function between two vector spaces that preserves vector addition and scalar multiplication, and the Jacobian matrix represents the linear transformation of a vector-valued function.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="input">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">Input refers to the values that are used to compute the output of a function, and the Jacobian matrix captures how these inputs are transformed into outputs.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="output">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">Output refers to the result of a function's operation on its inputs, and the Jacobian matrix describes the mapping from inputs to outputs.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="partial derivative">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
  <data key="d1">Partial derivative is the derivative of a multivariable function with respect to one variable, while the other variables are held constant, and they are used to construct the Jacobian matrix.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</node>
<node id="multi-dimensional function">
  <data key="d0">mathematical concept</data>
