</node>
<node id="计算近端算子">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A category referring to an optimization technique used in mathematical computations involving the proximity operator. The technique deals with minimizing a function that is a sum of norms. It is related to the Lasso problem and iterative methods such as Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="Lasso 问题">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">Lasso problem is a type of optimization problem in statistics and machine learning, which involves minimizing a loss function subject to a constraint on the sum of the absolute values of the coefficients. It is used for feature selection and regularization in linear regression.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="Jacobi 和 Gauss-Seidel 方法">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel methods are iterative methods used to solve systems of linear equations. They are numerical algorithms that use different strategies to update variables in the system. They are commonly used for solving linear systems where the matrix is split into diagonal, lower triangular, and upper triangular matrices.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="谱半径">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">Spectral radius is a measure that determines the convergence of iterative methods, calculated as the maximum absolute value of the eigenvalues of a matrix.&lt;SEP&gt;Spectral radius is mentioned as a measure of matrix eigenvalues, but no specific matrix or its spectral radius is identified in the text.&lt;SEP&gt;The spectral radius of a matrix is the largest magnitude of its eigenvalues. It is a measure of the convergence rate of iterative methods like Jacobi and Gauss-Seidel, as it indicates how quickly the iteration process will converge to a solution or divergence from a solution.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff7dec96cfa121a0e11b325e13ce1fa</data>
</node>
<node id="收敛速度">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">Convergence speed refers to the rate at which an iterative method approaches a solution. It is a measure of how quickly the method will find the correct answer to a problem, with a higher speed indicating faster convergence.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id=" prox⋅(b) ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical notation indicating the proximity operator applied to a variable b, which is used in optimization problems involving norms and minimization of a function. It is a key concept in the text and represents the optimization process.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="arg min ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical notation indicating the process of finding the minimum value of a function. It is used in optimization problems to identify the optimal solution, which is the variable x in the given context.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="‖x − b‖ ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical notation representing the norm of the difference between variables x and b, which is part of the objective function in optimization problems. It measures the distance between x and b.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="‖x‖ ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical notation representing the norm of variable x, which is also part of the objective function in optimization problems. It measures the magnitude of x.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="2λ ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A constant multiplier in the objective function, where λ is a parameter that influences the trade-off between the two norms in the function.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="x^* ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical notation indicating the optimal solution x^* for the given optimization problem. It represents the value of x that minimizes the objective function.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="b^ ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical notation indicating a component of the vector b, which is involved in the proximity operator calculation.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="x^ ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical notation indicating a component of the vector x, which is involved in the proximity operator calculation and optimization process.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="sgn(b) ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical function representing the sign function of b, which returns 1 if b is positive, -1 if b is negative, and 0 if b is zero. It is used to determine the sign of b in the proximity operator formula.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="max{">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical function that returns the larger of two values. It is used in the proximity operator formula to ensure that the resulting x^* is non-negative.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="|b| ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical notation representing the absolute value of b, which is used in the proximity operator formula to calculate x^*.</data>
  <data key="d2">chunk-47d799488921d39a7d144085504292d8</data>
</node>
<node id="λ ">
  <data key="d0">category</data>
  <data key="d1">A mathematical parameter in the objective function that affects the optimization process and the resulting solution x^*.</data>
