<edge source="Jacobian Matrix" target="vector-valued function">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">The Jacobian matrix is used to represent the linear transformation induced by a vector-valued function on its input space.</data>
  <data key="d5">transformation, input space</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Jacobian Matrix" target="input space">
  <data key="d3">7.0</data>
  <data key="d4">The Jacobian matrix describes how the input space of a function is transformed into the output space.</data>
  <data key="d5">mapping, transformation</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="Jacobian Matrix" target="output space">
  <data key="d3">7.0</data>
  <data key="d4">The Jacobian matrix captures the linear mapping from input space to output space, which is important for understanding the function's behavior.</data>
  <data key="d5">mapping, output space</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="input space" target="m × n matrix">
  <data key="d3">7.0</data>
  <data key="d4">The m × n matrix defines the input space of a linear transformation, specifying the dimensions and structure of the input vectors.</data>
  <data key="d5">dimension, input space</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="output space" target="m × n matrix">
  <data key="d3">7.0</data>
  <data key="d4">The m × n matrix defines the output space of a linear transformation, specifying the dimensions and structure of the output vectors.</data>
  <data key="d5">dimension, output space</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="derivative" target="gradient">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">The derivative of a function can be represented as a gradient, which points in the direction of the steepest increase of the function.</data>
