</edge>
<edge source="gradient" target="marginal change">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">The gradient of a function indicates the direction and magnitude of the marginal change in the function, which can be calculated using the Jacobian matrix.</data>
  <data key="d5">measurement, change</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="gradient" target="partial derivatives">
  <data key="d3">9.0</data>
  <data key="d4">The gradient is formed by the partial derivatives of a function, and the Jacobian matrix represents the gradient as a vector of partial derivatives.</data>
  <data key="d5">formation, representation</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="gradient" target="level set">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">The gradient of a function is perpendicular to the level set of that function, which represents the set of points where the function has a constant value.</data>
  <data key="d5">perpendicularity, constant value</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-2204ffef1e97e333dfc5498edbd5ae7b</data>
</edge>
<edge source="m × n matrix" target="linear transformation">
  <data key="d3">7.0</data>
  <data key="d4">A m × n matrix can represent a linear transformation, and the Jacobian matrix is an example of such a transformation, capturing the behavior of a function in a linear manner.</data>
  <data key="d5">transformation, linear</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="m × n matrix" target="output variable">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">Each output variable is represented by a row in the m × n matrix, indicating the rate of change of the function with respect to each input variable.</data>
  <data key="d5">representation, change rate</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="output variable" target="function">
  <data key="d3">8.0</data>
  <data key="d4">Each output variable is a result of the function acting on the input variables, and the Jacobian matrix describes the relationship between the inputs and outputs.</data>
  <data key="d5">functionality, relationship</data>
  <data key="d6">chunk-a54b0aaa2a6a35baa49d98df4ff00b5c</data>
</edge>
<edge source="function" target="input variable">
